.TH date "" "Command"
.SH NAME
date \- print/set date and time of day
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBdate [\-u] [\fIyymmddhhmm\fB[\fI.ss\fB]]\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.HS
date -- print/set date and time of day
Usage:	date [ -u ] [ yymmddhhmm[.ss] ]
Option:	-u	Print (and enter) date in Greenwich Mean Time
.HE
With no argument,
.B date
prints the current date and time, followed by the time zone name.
.PP
With a numeric argument,
.B date
sets the system's date and time (only the superuser may do this) and
then prints the result exactly as it would with no argument.
The argument gives, from the right, the minute and hour (\fImm\fR and
\fIhh\fR, both required), and optionally the day of month, month, and
last two digits of the year (\fIdd\fR, \fImm\fR, \fIyy\fR), each
optional and taken two digits at a time working leftward; any of the
optional leading fields that are omitted are left at their current
value.
A seconds field (\fI.ss\fR) may follow the minutes field.
.B date
refuses to set an impossible date, such as day 32 of a month, or a
month that has fewer days than the given day of month allows.
.IP \fB\-u\fR
Print and, if setting the time, interpret the argument in Greenwich
Mean Time instead of the local time zone.
.PP
When it sets the time,
.B date
also records the change in
.B /usr/adm/wtmp
and adjusts the modification time of
.B /etc/boottime
so that the recorded system-up-since time stays consistent across the
change.
.SH FILES
.DS
.ta 1.6i
\fB/usr/adm/wtmp\fR	login/time-change accounting file
\fB/etc/boottime\fR	holds the system boot time
.DE
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.B who
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.B date
prints a usage message and exits with status 1 if given an option
other than
.BR \-u ,
more than one non-option argument, an argument whose minute or hour
field is not two digits, or a day of month that does not exist in the
given (or current) month.
It also exits with status 1, after printing ``No permission'', if a
non-superuser tries to set the time.
.SH NOTES
Only the superuser can change the system's date or time.
.PP
.B date
builds its printed output from the C library's
.BR asctime() ,
which ends with the four-digit year, followed by the time zone name;
this places the year immediately before the zone name in
.BR date 's
output, rather than after it as in some other systems' date commands.
